window.QELVIORA_LEARNING = [{"slug":"portfolio","name":"Portfolio","category":"Career","description":"Present your work clearly to employers and collaborators.","project":"Prepare a project case study with a README, screenshots and runnable instructions.","question":"What makes a portfolio credible?","answer":"Clear evidence, your contribution and honest explanations of results and limitations.","lessons":[{"title":"Choose one project and define its purpose","explanation":"A portfolio should show how you solve a concrete problem, not just list technologies. Choose a project you can run, explain and improve; a small completed application is often easier to assess than a large unfinished idea.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Explain your own contribution and decisions","explanation":"Describe the problem, your role and the choices you made. Separate your contribution from team work and state limitations so a reviewer can understand what the project actually demonstrates.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Organise source files and remove sensitive data","explanation":"Organise the repository so its entry points and important files are easy to find. Remove credentials, private data and unrelated generated files before sharing it.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Write setup instructions with expected results","explanation":"A useful README lets a new reader reproduce the demonstration. Include prerequisites, setup, configuration, run commands, screenshots and the behaviour they should expect.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Provide a working demonstration where appropriate","explanation":"A demonstration link should work without relying on your development machine. Check the main workflow, small screens and any access restrictions before sharing it.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Review the portfolio from a first-time visitor’s perspective","explanation":"Review your portfolio as if you had never seen the project. Ask someone to find its purpose and run it; update unclear instructions and broken links before sending it to employers.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"claude-code","name":"Claude Code","category":"AI","description":"Use an AI coding assistant with clear context and careful review.","project":"Use an assistant to make one small project change and document your review.","question":"Should generated code be accepted without review?","answer":"No. Check correctness, permissions, security and behaviour before using it.","lessons":[{"title":"Describe project structure and development constraints","explanation":"An AI coding tool needs enough context to understand a project’s structure and constraints. Supply the task, relevant files, existing conventions and the behaviour that must be preserved.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Break a change into reviewable steps","explanation":"Divide a change into small steps with observable outcomes. A clear plan makes it easier to inspect whether the generated work stays within scope.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Review the permissions needed for a task","explanation":"Permissions decide what the assistant can read, change or execute. Give access appropriate to the task and inspect consequential operations rather than assuming every request is necessary.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Give focused requests and relevant context","explanation":"A focused request describes the desired behaviour and acceptance criteria. Relevant examples are usually more useful than a vague request to make everything better.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Inspect changes before accepting them","explanation":"Inspect the diff, not just the assistant’s summary. Check changed interfaces, error handling, dependencies and unexpected file modifications.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Run checks and document the resulting behaviour","explanation":"Run the checks that verify the changed behaviour. Record what was tested and what remains uncertain, and commit only the changes you understand.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"linkedin","name":"LinkedIn","category":"Career","description":"Make your professional profile clear and relevant.","project":"Draft a profile summary and one project description.","question":"What should support a claimed skill?","answer":"A specific example of work or learning that you can explain.","lessons":[{"title":"Write a headline describing your direction","explanation":"Your headline should help a reader understand the kind of work you want to do. Use accurate skills and a clear direction instead of unsupported claims or a long list of unrelated roles.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Explain skills with concrete examples","explanation":"Describe skills through work you have performed. A short project example with your contribution is more useful than an adjective such as expert.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Add projects that demonstrate your contribution","explanation":"Project entries should explain the problem, implementation and result. Include a link only when you have checked that it is usable by the intended reader.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Keep experience and education accurate","explanation":"Use accurate dates, roles and educational details. Consistency between your profile and résumé makes the information easier to evaluate.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Write concise, relevant outreach messages","explanation":"Relevant outreach explains why you are contacting someone and what you are asking for. Keep it short, specific and respectful; avoid sending the same generic pitch to everyone.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Review your profile for clarity and consistency","explanation":"Read your profile from the perspective of a recruiter or collaborator. Remove duplication, check links and make your best relevant evidence easy to find.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"git-github","name":"Git & GitHub","category":"Full Stack","description":"Organise project history and collaborate through reviewable changes.","project":"Publish a practice project with a branch and a reviewed change.","question":"What belongs in a focused commit?","answer":"One coherent change that can be understood and reviewed independently.","lessons":[{"title":"Inspect modified files before committing","explanation":"Before committing, inspect the files that have changed and the diff for each one. This helps separate intentional edits from temporary output or accidental modifications.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Create focused commits with useful messages","explanation":"A commit should capture one coherent step. Its message should explain the purpose of the change so someone can follow the history later.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Use branches for separate changes","explanation":"A branch gives a separate line of development for a change. Keep its purpose clear and update it carefully as the shared project evolves.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Review and resolve conflicting edits carefully","explanation":"A conflict means different edits overlap. Read both intentions, choose the correct combined behaviour and run relevant checks after resolving it.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Document setup and usage in the repository","explanation":"A repository needs instructions that let another person reproduce the work. Document configuration, dependencies and run steps rather than assuming they share your environment.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Keep credentials and generated clutter out of version control","explanation":"Do not commit passwords, API keys or private records. Use appropriate configuration files outside source control and review history if a secret was accidentally included.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"javascript","name":"JavaScript","category":"Frontend","description":"Build interactive interfaces using clear programming foundations.","project":"Build a searchable catalogue using sample data.","question":"Why handle a failed request?","answer":"To explain the problem and let the user recover instead of showing a broken interface.","lessons":[{"title":"Understand values, types and comparisons","explanation":"Values have types that influence operations and comparisons. Check what data a function receives and prefer explicit conversions when input arrives as text.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Use functions to organise repeated operations","explanation":"A function expresses a repeatable operation through its inputs and result. Keep its responsibility clear and avoid changing unrelated state unexpectedly.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Transform arrays and access object data","explanation":"Arrays represent sequences; objects associate properties with values. Choose an operation such as map, filter or lookup that matches the transformation you need.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Handle events and update the interface","explanation":"An event handler connects a user action to application behaviour. Read the input, validate it and update the interface so the result is visible.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Manage asynchronous requests and failures","explanation":"Asynchronous work may complete later or fail. Handle loading and rejection, and avoid showing a success state before the operation actually succeeds.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Split reusable logic into modules","explanation":"Modules separate reusable behaviour from the page entry point. Use clear exports and keep dependencies understandable so the project remains easy to change.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"ui-ux","name":"UI/UX","category":"Design","description":"Make product interfaces easier to understand and use.","project":"Redesign a confusing enquiry form and explain each improvement.","question":"Why design an empty state?","answer":"It explains what is missing and helps users take the next useful step.","lessons":[{"title":"Identify the visitor’s main task","explanation":"Start by naming the user’s task and success condition. An interface should help that task happen with as little avoidable uncertainty as possible.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Arrange content using a clear hierarchy","explanation":"Visual hierarchy guides attention through size, spacing and placement. Give the primary information and action more emphasis than secondary details.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Choose readable type, spacing and contrast","explanation":"Readable type and adequate contrast help people understand the content. Check the actual page at smaller sizes rather than assuming a style token guarantees usability.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Design keyboard-friendly controls","explanation":"Interactive elements need clear labels, keyboard access and visible focus. Use semantic controls so their behaviour is understandable to assistive technology.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Plan loading, empty and error states","explanation":"The interface must explain waiting, missing data and failures. A useful state tells the user what happened and what they can do next.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Review the interface with representative tasks","explanation":"Review the interface through a real task, such as finding a course and sending an enquiry. Observe points of confusion and revise the layout or wording.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"rest-apis","name":"REST APIs","category":"Backend","description":"Design predictable interfaces between applications.","project":"Design an API for courses and enquiries.","question":"What should an API error tell a client?","answer":"What failed, a useful status, and enough information to correct a valid request.","lessons":[{"title":"Name resources consistently","explanation":"Resources describe the things an API exposes, such as courses or enquiries. Consistent naming lets clients predict related routes without guessing.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Choose methods that match the operation","explanation":"Choose a method based on the operation: retrieval, creation, replacement or another defined action. Document the intended behaviour instead of relying on a route’s name alone.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Validate requests and required fields","explanation":"Validate required fields, types and allowed values before processing a request. Client-side checks do not replace server-side validation.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Return meaningful status codes and errors","explanation":"A response should provide a meaningful status and useful content. Errors should explain the problem without exposing credentials or internal implementation details.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Define pagination for larger collections","explanation":"Large collections need a consistent paging approach. State how clients request the next part and what ordering or filtering rules apply.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document example requests and responses","explanation":"Document a working request and its expected response. Include validation failures so callers can understand how to recover.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"sql","name":"SQL","category":"Backend","description":"Store structured information and query it reliably.","project":"Create a practice database with courses and enquiries.","question":"Why use query parameters?","answer":"They separate input data from SQL instructions and help prevent injection.","lessons":[{"title":"Model records with tables and keys","explanation":"Tables model entities, while keys identify records and connect relationships. Design around the questions and updates the application needs to support.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Use constraints to protect data integrity","explanation":"Constraints protect assumptions such as unique identifiers and required values. Enforcing them in storage helps preserve integrity even when application code changes.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Filter and order query results","explanation":"Filtering selects records that satisfy a condition; ordering defines how results are presented. Make both explicit when a user relies on the result sequence.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Join related tables when needed","explanation":"A join combines related rows through a matching rule. Check relationship cardinality so one record is not unexpectedly duplicated.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Aggregate values and check totals","explanation":"Aggregations produce summaries such as counts and totals. Check which rows are included and how missing values affect the calculation.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Use parameterised inputs and inspect query plans","explanation":"Query parameters separate data from SQL instructions. Use them for untrusted inputs and inspect execution plans when performance needs investigation.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"authentication","name":"Authentication","category":"Backend","description":"Understand identity and secure access in application development.","project":"Draw and review an authentication flow for a fictional app.","question":"Does signing in automatically grant all permissions?","answer":"No. Each protected operation still requires appropriate authorisation.","lessons":[{"title":"Distinguish identification, authentication and authorisation","explanation":"Authentication establishes who is making a request; authorisation decides what they may do. These are separate decisions that protected workflows must enforce.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Use established password-hashing approaches","explanation":"Use established password-hashing implementations instead of storing plain passwords or inventing cryptography. Password verification must not reveal the stored secret.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Understand sessions and tokens","explanation":"A session or token represents an authenticated interaction. Its creation, expiry and revocation need a clear policy appropriate to the application.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Review secure cookie and expiry settings","explanation":"Cookie settings influence whether session information can be read by scripts or sent in cross-site requests. Review flags and transport requirements as part of the design.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Control access to protected operations","explanation":"Protect operations on the server, not only by hiding buttons. Verify the caller and the resource permissions before changing protected data.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Check logout, expired sessions and rejected requests","explanation":"Check expired credentials, logout and unauthorised requests as well as successful sign-in. Failures should reject access without leaking sensitive information.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"typescript","name":"TypeScript","category":"Frontend","description":"Use types to express contracts and catch mistakes earlier.","project":"Add types to a small JavaScript data-processing feature.","question":"Do static types validate external input at runtime?","answer":"No. External data still needs runtime validation.","lessons":[{"title":"Describe values with suitable types","explanation":"A type describes the values a variable or interface is intended to accept. Use a specific type when it conveys a useful contract.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Model objects and function inputs","explanation":"Object and function types document expected fields, arguments and results. These contracts make changes easier to check across module boundaries.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Use unions and narrow uncertain values","explanation":"A union allows several alternatives; narrowing establishes which one is present before using its specific properties. Check uncertain data before assuming a shape.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Type component or API boundaries","explanation":"An API or component boundary should state what it needs and returns. Keep these types aligned with the actual behaviour and data model.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Avoid hiding problems with unnecessary any types","explanation":"Using any removes useful checks from that part of the program. Prefer an explicit shape or unknown with validation when the value is uncertain.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Check the project and handle runtime input separately","explanation":"TypeScript checks the program before execution; it does not automatically validate external data. Parse and validate requests or downloaded content at runtime.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"react","name":"React","category":"Frontend","description":"Compose interactive interfaces from reusable components.","project":"Build a filterable task or course list.","question":"Why use stable list keys?","answer":"They help React identify items correctly when a list changes.","lessons":[{"title":"Break the interface into focused components","explanation":"A component should represent a clear part of the interface. Divide the page by responsibility rather than making every small visual detail a separate abstraction.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Pass data using props","explanation":"Props provide data from a parent to a child. Treat incoming data as a contract and avoid mutating it inside the child.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Keep changing interface data in state","explanation":"State stores changing information that affects rendering. Keep it close to the components that need it and derive values that do not need their own state.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Render lists with stable keys","explanation":"Stable keys let React identify list items between renders. A key should follow the identity of the item rather than its current position.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Handle forms and user events","explanation":"Form handlers connect user input to state and validation. Explain failures and avoid disabling useful corrections after an invalid submission.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Use effects only when synchronising with external systems","explanation":"Effects are for synchronising with something outside rendering, such as a subscription. Avoid using them merely to calculate data that can be derived during render.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"next-js","name":"Next.js","category":"Frontend","description":"Organise a web application with routes and server-aware rendering.","project":"Build a small catalogue with a list and a detail route.","question":"Where should a secret API key be used?","answer":"On the server, rather than in browser-delivered code.","lessons":[{"title":"Map the application’s pages and routes","explanation":"A route connects a URL to a page or operation. Plan the list, detail and error experiences before adding navigation links.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Separate server work from client interactions","explanation":"Server work can access trusted configuration and private data sources. Client components handle browser interactions; keep that boundary deliberate.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Fetch and validate application data","explanation":"Validate data at the boundary where it enters the application. A successful fetch still needs an appropriate response shape and error strategy.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Handle loading and errors in the route flow","explanation":"Loading and error behaviour are part of the route experience. A user should be able to understand waiting and recover from a failed operation.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Keep secrets outside browser code","explanation":"Browser-delivered code cannot safely contain a secret API key. Keep provider calls that require secrets on the server.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Prepare and check a working deployment","explanation":"Check the built application in its deployment environment. Confirm the important routes and configuration rather than relying solely on a development preview.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"tailwind-css","name":"Tailwind CSS","category":"Frontend","description":"Create consistent layouts through reusable styling decisions.","project":"Style a responsive catalogue with clear cards and forms.","question":"What makes a consistent visual system?","answer":"Shared decisions for spacing, type, colour and interaction states.","lessons":[{"title":"Understand utility-based styling","explanation":"Utilities describe small styling decisions directly at the component. Read the resulting layout as a whole so local choices form a coherent interface.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Apply spacing and typography consistently","explanation":"Consistent spacing and typography create predictable rhythm. Define a small set of repeated decisions instead of giving every element a unique treatment.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Build responsive layout variations","explanation":"Responsive styling should reflect how content rearranges, not merely shrink text. Test long labels, forms and navigation at small widths.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Design visible focus and interaction states","explanation":"Focus and hover are different interaction states. Provide visible keyboard focus and maintain readable contrast in each state.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Keep class combinations understandable","explanation":"Keep repeated class combinations understandable. A small reusable component may help when a pattern has a clear semantic purpose.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Review repeated patterns for consistency","explanation":"Review related components together. Similar controls should look and behave consistently, including their disabled, loading and error states.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"express","name":"Express","category":"Backend","description":"Build server-side routes with predictable request handling.","project":"Build an enquiry API using fictional data.","question":"Why centralise error handling?","answer":"It supports consistent responses and avoids leaking internal details.","lessons":[{"title":"Organise routes and middleware","explanation":"Routes identify request endpoints; middleware handles shared processing around them. Organise both so their responsibilities are easy to inspect.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Parse and validate request bodies","explanation":"Parsing a request body only produces data; it does not prove that data is valid. Check required fields and reject unsupported inputs.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Separate business logic from transport handling","explanation":"Separate application rules from request transport where it improves clarity. This lets a rule be checked without constructing an entire server request.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Connect routes to application data","explanation":"Data access should use clear interfaces and safe inputs. Return controlled results rather than exposing storage implementation details.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Handle errors centrally and safely","explanation":"Central error handling can keep responses consistent. Log useful internal context safely while sending clients an appropriate message.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document configuration and API usage","explanation":"Document the configuration and commands needed to run the API. Include example requests that another developer can reproduce.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"full-stack","name":"Full-Stack","category":"Full Stack","description":"Connect a working interface, API and data layer.","project":"Create a fictional course enquiry tracker.","question":"What should a complete application test cover?","answer":"The end-to-end user behaviour, including validation and failure states.","lessons":[{"title":"Define the smallest useful user workflow","explanation":"Define one useful flow before choosing features. A small complete journey makes it easier to understand what frontend, API and storage must each provide.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Model the data needed for the workflow","explanation":"Model the information required by that flow. Avoid collecting or storing data that has no defined role in the application.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Build an interface with clear states","explanation":"The interface should show loading, success, empty and error states. Make the primary workflow understandable even when something goes wrong.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Create and validate API operations","explanation":"APIs enforce application rules and validate inputs. The browser’s form checks support usability but cannot protect server data on their own.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Connect the interface to application storage","explanation":"Connect each interface operation to a real data operation. Check that a saved value is actually available again instead of reporting success based on a visual change.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test and document the complete journey","explanation":"Test the journey from input to result and document how to reproduce it. Include both the expected path and meaningful failures.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"deployment","name":"Deployment","category":"Full Stack","description":"Prepare an application for use outside development.","project":"Deploy a static portfolio and verify its links on a phone.","question":"Why check the deployed application?","answer":"Production configuration and runtime conditions can differ from development.","lessons":[{"title":"Choose a deployment approach for the stack","explanation":"Choose a hosting approach that supports the application’s runtime. A static export and a server-backed application have different requirements.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Separate configuration from source code","explanation":"Configuration should be separate from source where environments differ. State which values are required without putting secrets in public files.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Protect secrets and database credentials","explanation":"Protect provider and database credentials using the host’s secret mechanisms. Review browser output so sensitive values are not bundled into it.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Verify build output and production settings","explanation":"A successful build must produce the files or entry point the host expects. Check output paths and runtime compatibility before uploading.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Check pages and key interactions after deployment","explanation":"Test the published page, important links and primary workflow. Confirm that production configuration matches the intended environment.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document updates and recovery steps","explanation":"Document how to update and recover the application. Keeping a known working version helps make future changes reviewable.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"python","name":"Python","category":"AI","description":"Write understandable programs for data and automation tasks.","project":"Create a command-line tool that summarises a sample dataset.","question":"Why isolate repeated logic in a function?","answer":"It makes the operation reusable, easier to test and clearer to call.","lessons":[{"title":"Use collections and control flow","explanation":"Collections and control flow organise a program’s decisions and data. Choose a representation that makes the intended operations straightforward.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Organise logic with functions and modules","explanation":"Functions give reusable behaviour a clear interface. Modules organise related code so entry points and dependencies are easier to understand.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Read and write structured files","explanation":"Reading a file requires assumptions about its format and encoding. Validate its structure and give a useful error when it cannot be processed.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Handle exceptions with useful messages","explanation":"Catch errors when you can explain or recover from them. Do not silently hide failures that change the result of the program.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Call APIs with validation and error handling","explanation":"An API request may fail or return unexpected data. Validate status and content before using the response.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document dependencies and how to run the tool","explanation":"Document the environment, dependencies and sample command. A small tool is more useful when another person can reproduce its result.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"machine-learning","name":"Machine Learning","category":"AI","description":"Learn predictive modelling through careful experiments.","project":"Compare baseline and model results on a practice dataset.","question":"Why keep test data separate?","answer":"To estimate behaviour on data that was not used to fit the model.","lessons":[{"title":"Define a prediction task and dataset","explanation":"A modelling task needs a defined target and a relevant dataset. Explain what a prediction means and how it could be evaluated.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Prepare features without leaking future information","explanation":"Features must only use information available at prediction time. Leakage can make results look strong while the real application fails.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Separate training and evaluation data","explanation":"Keep evaluation data separate from data used to fit the model. The split should reflect the conditions in which the model will be used.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Compare a simple baseline with a model","explanation":"A simple baseline shows whether a more complex approach adds value. Compare methods using the same evaluation conditions.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Choose metrics relevant to the task","explanation":"Choose a metric that represents important errors in the task. Accuracy alone may conceal failures in an imbalanced dataset.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Inspect errors and explain limitations","explanation":"Inspect mistakes and describe limitations. A useful report explains when the model should not be trusted as well as where it performs well.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"pandas","name":"Pandas","category":"Data","description":"Prepare and summarise tabular datasets.","project":"Clean a fictional sales dataset and summarise revenue by category.","question":"Why check row counts after a join?","answer":"Unexpected matches can multiply or drop records and distort results.","lessons":[{"title":"Inspect column types and sample records","explanation":"Inspect column names, types and sample rows before changing data. This helps reveal assumptions about units, dates and missing values.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Find missing values and inconsistent formats","explanation":"Cleaning needs explicit rules for inconsistent or missing entries. Document the rule so the result can be reproduced and questioned.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Remove duplicates with a stated rule","explanation":"Duplicates may be genuine repeated events or erroneous copies. Decide how records are identified before removing them.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Join related datasets carefully","explanation":"Check join keys and expected relationships before combining datasets. Compare row counts and totals after the join.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Group records and calculate useful summaries","explanation":"Grouping transforms records into summaries. State the grouping, measure and handling of missing data so readers know what the result represents.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Check the final data and document transformations","explanation":"Validate the prepared dataset against expected ranges and totals. Keep an account of transformations rather than treating cleaned data as unquestionable.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"model-evaluation","name":"Model Evaluation","category":"AI","description":"Measure model performance without misleading conclusions.","project":"Write an evaluation report for a practice classifier.","question":"When can accuracy be misleading?","answer":"When class imbalance or different error costs hide important mistakes.","lessons":[{"title":"Choose a baseline for comparison","explanation":"A baseline supplies a useful point of comparison. Without it, a metric does not show whether the model improves on a simple approach.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Use appropriate validation splits","explanation":"Validation should resemble future use. A random split may be unsuitable when time or related records create dependencies.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Select metrics for the business question","explanation":"Different metrics represent different error costs. Choose measures that fit the question and explain what they do not capture.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Inspect confusion matrices where relevant","explanation":"A confusion matrix shows which classes are confused. Use it to inspect the types of mistakes rather than only one overall score.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Check leakage, imbalance and overfitting","explanation":"Leakage, imbalance and overfitting can distort reported performance. Look for unintended information and compare training with held-out results.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Report uncertainty and failure cases","explanation":"Report the evaluation conditions, dataset limits and important failures. Avoid implying that one test establishes reliability in every situation.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"prompt-engineering","name":"Prompt Engineering","category":"AI","description":"Design clearer tasks and evaluate generated responses.","project":"Compare prompts for summarising a fictional project brief.","question":"Why test prompts against several examples?","answer":"A prompt that works once may fail on other inputs or edge cases.","lessons":[{"title":"State the task and desired outcome","explanation":"Start with the task, intended audience and success condition. A model cannot reliably infer every requirement from a vague request.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Provide relevant context and constraints","explanation":"Relevant context helps the model answer the actual problem. Include needed facts while avoiding unrelated material or confidential information.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Specify a useful response format","explanation":"A response format should support how the result will be used. State required fields or structure, then verify the produced result.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Test with representative examples","explanation":"Test prompts on several representative inputs. Include ambiguous or incomplete cases to reveal limitations.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Inspect failures and revise instructions","explanation":"Compare failures against the criteria and revise the instruction or workflow. More wording is not automatically a better prompt.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Protect sensitive information in the workflow","explanation":"Treat prompts and responses as information sent through the chosen service. Use non-sensitive examples while learning and review output before acting on it.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"llm-apis","name":"LLM APIs","category":"AI","description":"Integrate language-model responses into an application.","project":"Build an AI-assisted summary feature using non-sensitive sample text.","question":"Should provider credentials be sent to the browser?","answer":"No. API requests using secrets belong on a trusted server.","lessons":[{"title":"Keep API credentials on the server","explanation":"API credentials should remain on a trusted server. The browser sends the visitor’s question to your endpoint, not the secret to the provider.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Define and validate the user request","explanation":"Validate message size, shape and allowed roles before sending a request. Set a bounded task rather than exposing unrestricted provider parameters.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Handle timeouts and upstream failures","explanation":"Requests can time out or fail. Return a useful fallback and avoid claiming that a response or action succeeded when it did not.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Extract the useful response content","explanation":"Read the response according to its schema. Extract the useful text or structured result while handling empty or unexpected output.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Monitor usage and output length","explanation":"Set suitable output limits and observe request usage. A public endpoint should have controls appropriate to its expected traffic.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Check generated output before acting on it","explanation":"Generated responses may be wrong even when the API call succeeds. Review important claims and validate structured output before using it.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"rag","name":"RAG","category":"AI","description":"Connect document retrieval with grounded answers.","project":"Design a document-answering demo with visible source references.","question":"Does retrieval guarantee a correct answer?","answer":"No. Retrieved material and generated claims still require evaluation.","lessons":[{"title":"Prepare documents for search","explanation":"Documents need a reliable preparation process before retrieval. Preserve the source identity so later answers can refer to supporting material.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Choose meaningful text segments","explanation":"Segments should contain enough context to be understandable but remain useful for search. Evaluate segmentation against actual questions.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Retrieve content relevant to the question","explanation":"Retrieval selects material relevant to the question. Inspect whether the returned passages actually contain evidence for an answer.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Generate an answer using source context","explanation":"The generation step should use the retrieved context and admit missing evidence. Do not treat unrelated source text as a valid answer.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Show references supporting the response","explanation":"Citations should identify the material supporting a claim. Check that each reference points to evidence rather than just naming a document.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test unsupported questions and weak retrieval","explanation":"Test questions that cannot be answered from the collection. A grounded system should make that limitation visible rather than inventing details.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"ai-agents","name":"AI Agents","category":"AI","description":"Build bounded workflows that can use tools.","project":"Plan an assistant that drafts a task and asks before taking action.","question":"Why give an agent explicit stopping rules?","answer":"They prevent uncontrolled retries and help keep work within the intended scope.","lessons":[{"title":"Define the goal and permitted actions","explanation":"Define the agent’s goal and boundaries before providing tools. Some workflows are better served by a fixed script with predictable steps.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Give tools narrow inputs and outputs","explanation":"Tools should expose specific operations with validated inputs. Narrow interfaces make behaviour easier to inspect and constrain.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Validate tool arguments and results","explanation":"A tool result may contain incomplete or untrusted information. Validate the result before using it as a basis for another action.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Require approval for consequential actions","explanation":"Consequential operations may require approval. Make the proposed action concrete so a person can understand what will happen.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Set stopping rules and recovery behaviour","explanation":"Retries and tool use need stopping conditions. Bound the workflow and explain when a missing input or failed operation prevents completion.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Compare the agent with a simpler fixed workflow","explanation":"Compare the agent’s result with a simpler approach. Use added autonomy only when it improves the task enough to justify its complexity.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"ai-coding-tools","name":"AI Coding Tools","category":"AI","description":"Use code generation while retaining ownership of changes.","project":"Review an AI-generated change and record accepted and rejected suggestions.","question":"Who remains responsible for generated changes?","answer":"The developer who reviews and integrates them.","lessons":[{"title":"Describe the task and project constraints","explanation":"Explain the required behaviour and existing conventions before asking for code. Relevant context reduces the chance of a technically plausible but unsuitable change.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Limit the scope of the requested change","explanation":"A bounded request is easier to review than a project-wide rewrite. Identify which behaviour should change and what must remain compatible.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Inspect generated code and dependencies","explanation":"Read generated changes for correctness, dependencies and unexpected effects. A fluent explanation is not evidence that the implementation works.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Check behaviour with meaningful tests","explanation":"Choose checks based on the requirement being changed. Avoid tests that merely repeat the implementation without verifying observable behaviour.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Keep secrets out of prompts and source","explanation":"Do not put credentials or private records in prompts or public source. Use fictional data when demonstrating an integration.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document decisions you can explain independently","explanation":"Be able to explain the resulting code and trade-offs. The developer remains responsible for integrating and maintaining generated work.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"solidity","name":"Solidity","category":"Web3","description":"Understand smart-contract logic through a small practice project.","project":"Write a small test-only contract with documented access rules.","question":"Why test rejected operations?","answer":"To confirm invalid or unauthorised actions cannot change protected state.","lessons":[{"title":"Model state and allowed operations","explanation":"Contract state models what persists between operations. Keep allowed state transitions and caller permissions clear.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Define access rules and validation","explanation":"Validate inputs and authorise operations before changing protected state. Visibility alone does not define who should be permitted to act.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Understand transaction execution and gas","explanation":"Transactions execute under resource and network constraints. Understand how a failure affects the operation before designing dependent steps.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Test expected and rejected operations","explanation":"Tests should cover expected behaviour and rejected calls. Edge cases are important because deployed contract logic can be difficult to change.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Review external calls and failure behaviour","explanation":"External calls introduce interactions with other code. Review ordering, failure handling and assumptions about the called contract.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Use a test environment before considering real deployment","explanation":"Use a test environment with no real funds while learning. Document what the demonstration does and avoid presenting it as audited production software.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"ethereum","name":"Ethereum","category":"Web3","description":"Understand how an application interacts with a blockchain.","project":"Build a test-network interface that reads a sample contract.","question":"Is a submitted transaction already confirmed?","answer":"No. Submission and confirmation are different states.","lessons":[{"title":"Distinguish accounts, contracts and networks","explanation":"Accounts, contracts and networks have distinct roles. Check which network and address the application is using before attempting an operation.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Connect an interface to a test wallet","explanation":"A wallet connection makes an account available to the interface; it does not prove that every requested operation is appropriate.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Read contract state without a transaction","explanation":"Reading contract state can be separate from sending a transaction. Make the difference clear in the interface.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Explain what a transaction requests","explanation":"Before requesting a signature or transaction, explain the intended action. Users need to understand what they are being asked to approve.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Show pending, confirmed and failed states","explanation":"Show submitted, pending, confirmed and failed states separately. Submission alone should not be presented as completed execution.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Keep test-only demonstrations separate from real funds","explanation":"Keep demonstrations on an appropriate test network and use test assets. Label limitations so a learner does not confuse the demo with a real financial workflow.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"flutter","name":"Flutter","category":"Mobile","description":"Build a mobile interface with clear application structure.","project":"Build a small mobile catalogue with a details screen.","question":"Why check the application on a device?","answer":"Screen size, input, performance and platform behaviour affect usability.","lessons":[{"title":"Organise screens using reusable widgets","explanation":"Widgets describe the mobile interface’s structure and presentation. Group related behaviour in reusable components with a clear purpose.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Manage state for user interactions","explanation":"State represents changing application information. Choose an approach that keeps updates understandable as the number of screens grows.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Create navigation between screens","explanation":"Navigation should let a user reach a screen and return predictably. Plan what data each screen needs before connecting them.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Load data with appropriate failure states","explanation":"Network data needs loading and error states. Keep the interface usable when connectivity or the response is unreliable.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Store suitable local application preferences","explanation":"Use local storage only for information appropriate to the device. Separate user preferences from records that require durable shared storage.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Check layout and behaviour on a device","explanation":"Test on a representative device or emulator. Screen size, keyboard behaviour and platform differences can expose problems hidden in a desktop view.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"docker","name":"Docker","category":"Full Stack","description":"Package an application with repeatable runtime configuration.","project":"Containerise a small practice API with setup instructions.","question":"How does an image differ from a container?","answer":"An image describes the packaged environment; a container is a running instance.","lessons":[{"title":"Describe the application’s runtime needs","explanation":"Packaging starts with understanding runtime dependencies and required configuration. Describe what the application needs to start successfully.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Create an image with a clear build process","explanation":"An image build should be reproducible from the project inputs. Keep build steps clear and avoid adding unrelated files.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Keep unnecessary files out of the image","explanation":"Excluding private files and unnecessary output makes an image easier to review. Secrets should not become part of its layers.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Configure environment variables safely","explanation":"Provide environment-specific values at runtime through an appropriate mechanism. Document required values without embedding credentials.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Connect application and database services","explanation":"Several services need defined connections and readiness behaviour. A running database container does not always mean it is ready to accept requests.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document how to start and stop the environment","explanation":"Give another developer a tested start command and useful troubleshooting notes. Include how to stop the environment cleanly.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"data-structures","name":"Data Structures","category":"Backend","description":"Choose data organisations that fit the problem.","project":"Create a small interview reference with implementations and examples.","question":"Why choose a structure before coding?","answer":"Its supported operations and costs influence the quality of the solution.","lessons":[{"title":"Compare arrays and linked structures","explanation":"A data structure supports particular operations with particular costs. Choose based on the operations the problem needs most often.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Use maps for key-based lookup","explanation":"Maps associate keys with values and support lookup patterns. Understand how missing keys and repeated updates should behave.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Understand stacks and queues","explanation":"Stacks and queues model different processing orders. Use the one that matches whether the most recent or earliest item should be handled next.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Explore trees, heaps and graphs","explanation":"Trees, heaps and graphs represent different relationships. State the property your algorithm relies on before choosing an implementation.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Describe time and space costs","explanation":"Time and space costs describe how work grows with input size. Compare costs alongside implementation simplicity and actual constraints.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test empty inputs and boundary cases","explanation":"Test empty collections, repeated values and boundary operations. These cases often reveal assumptions hidden in a normal example.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"algorithms","name":"Algorithms","category":"Backend","description":"Solve common problems with explainable approaches.","project":"Solve a selection of practice problems and explain each approach.","question":"What should precede optimisation?","answer":"A correct solution and a clear understanding of its constraints.","lessons":[{"title":"Clarify inputs, outputs and constraints","explanation":"Clarify what the input represents and what the output must satisfy. Examples are useful, but constraints define which approaches are feasible.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Start with a correct simple solution","explanation":"Begin with a correct, understandable solution. This establishes a reference before attempting a more efficient method.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Recognise search, sorting and traversal patterns","explanation":"Common patterns such as binary search, traversal or two pointers fit specific conditions. Verify those conditions instead of applying a pattern by name.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Use recursion or iteration deliberately","explanation":"Recursion needs a base case and progress toward it. Iteration may be clearer when state transitions can be expressed directly.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Explain complexity and trade-offs","explanation":"Explain complexity in terms of input size and dominant operations. Include storage costs and important trade-offs.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test edge cases and document the reasoning","explanation":"Use examples that stress boundaries, duplicates and unusual inputs. Document why the algorithm still satisfies the output requirements.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"oop","name":"OOP","category":"Backend","description":"Model software responsibilities through objects and interfaces.","project":"Model a fictional booking workflow using a small set of classes.","question":"Why avoid overly coupled classes?","answer":"Changes become harder to isolate, review and test.","lessons":[{"title":"Define cohesive classes and responsibilities","explanation":"A class should group state and behaviour around a cohesive responsibility. Avoid giving one object unrelated duties simply because the data is nearby.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Encapsulate implementation details","explanation":"Encapsulation separates the public interface from internal implementation. Callers should depend on the contract rather than manipulating hidden details.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Choose composition or inheritance carefully","explanation":"Composition connects collaborating objects; inheritance describes a more specific form of an existing abstraction. Choose based on the relationship you need.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Use interfaces for suitable boundaries","explanation":"Interfaces make required behaviour explicit at a boundary. Keep them small enough that implementations do not need unrelated methods.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Avoid tight coupling between components","explanation":"Strong coupling makes a change spread across several components. Prefer clear responsibilities and explicit dependencies where they improve maintainability.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test behaviour through clear public interfaces","explanation":"Test the behaviour users of the class rely on. The test should remain useful when internal organisation changes without altering the contract.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"design-patterns","name":"Design Patterns","category":"Backend","description":"Use established structures when they solve a real design problem.","project":"Refactor a small feature and justify whether a pattern helps.","question":"Should every feature use a design pattern?","answer":"No. Apply a pattern only when its benefit outweighs the complexity.","lessons":[{"title":"Identify the recurring problem first","explanation":"A design pattern is a response to a recurring design problem. Name the problem and constraints before deciding whether the pattern fits.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Compare a simple solution with a pattern","explanation":"Compare the pattern with the simplest correct implementation. Added abstraction is useful only when it improves the actual design.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Explore creation and composition approaches","explanation":"Creation patterns organise how objects are produced; structural patterns organise relationships. Understand which part of the problem is changing.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Separate changing behaviour behind interfaces","explanation":"An interface can isolate varying behaviour from its caller. Keep the boundary tied to a real need rather than predicting every possible future variation.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Refactor without changing required behaviour","explanation":"Refactoring changes structure while preserving required behaviour. Use checks that reveal an unintended behavioural change.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Explain the added complexity and benefit","explanation":"Explain both the benefit and cost of the chosen structure. A design that is easy to name is not automatically easier to maintain.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"system-design","name":"System Design","category":"Backend","description":"Explain the structure and trade-offs of a practical system.","project":"Sketch a fictional appointment platform and discuss bottlenecks.","question":"What should drive an architecture decision?","answer":"The system’s requirements, constraints and expected operating conditions.","lessons":[{"title":"Clarify expected users and core workflows","explanation":"System design begins with the workload and user requirements. Ask what must work, at what scale and under which constraints.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Identify components and data boundaries","explanation":"Components have responsibilities and data boundaries. Explain how information moves between them instead of only drawing labelled boxes.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Choose APIs and storage needs","explanation":"APIs and storage choices should support the required access patterns. A familiar technology is not a substitute for understanding the need.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Consider caching and background work","explanation":"Caching and background work can improve performance but add freshness and failure questions. State the assumptions they require.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Plan failures and operational visibility","explanation":"Plan what happens when a dependency fails or slows down. Operational visibility should help identify the problem without exposing secrets.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Explain trade-offs and growth constraints","explanation":"Present the alternatives and why one was chosen. Discuss which changes in requirements would make a different design appropriate.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"testing","name":"Testing","category":"Full Stack","description":"Verify important behaviour at useful boundaries.","project":"Create tests for a form and its API.","question":"What makes a test useful?","answer":"It verifies meaningful behaviour and can reveal a real regression.","lessons":[{"title":"Choose requirements worth checking","explanation":"Useful tests verify requirements that could regress. Begin with the behaviour users or other components depend on.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Write focused tests for application logic","explanation":"Focused logic tests are easier to interpret when their inputs and expected outputs are explicit. Avoid relying on unrelated shared state.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Check APIs and validation boundaries","explanation":"Boundary tests check the contract between components, including accepted and rejected inputs. They complement internal logic checks.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Cover errors and edge cases","explanation":"Errors and edge cases need defined behaviour. Test that failure is handled predictably, not merely that an exception can occur.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Keep tests independent and repeatable","explanation":"Independent tests should run reliably in different orders. Use controlled data and avoid depending on a previous test’s side effects.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Use automated checks when changes are made","explanation":"Automated checks support review when code changes. Their value comes from meaningful coverage, not simply from having a large test count.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"sql-for-analytics","name":"SQL for Analytics","category":"Data","description":"Answer business questions using structured queries.","project":"Analyse fictional product activity using queries and a short report.","question":"Why define a metric before querying?","answer":"Different definitions can produce different answers to the same business question.","lessons":[{"title":"Define a clear metric and time window","explanation":"A business metric needs a definition, time window and population. Agree on these before writing a query.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Filter records using consistent rules","explanation":"Filtering rules determine which events or records count. Check dates, statuses and missing values that may change inclusion.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Aggregate and compare relevant groups","explanation":"An aggregation must match the level of analysis. Grouping by customer and grouping by event answer different questions.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Use joins without double-counting","explanation":"Joins can duplicate observations when relationships are not one-to-one. Validate the resulting population before calculating totals.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Explore ranking and window calculations","explanation":"Window calculations preserve individual rows while comparing related records. Use explicit ordering and partitions appropriate to the question.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Validate totals and explain limitations","explanation":"Check totals against a simpler query or known sample. Explain limitations so a reader does not infer more than the data supports.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"statistics","name":"Statistics","category":"Data","description":"Understand data variation and the limits of evidence.","project":"Compare two fictional groups and explain what the data cannot establish.","question":"Does correlation prove causation?","answer":"No. Confounding variables or chance can explain an association.","lessons":[{"title":"Compare typical values with mean and median","explanation":"Mean and median describe typical values differently. Outliers and skew can make one summary more representative than the other.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Describe spread and unusual observations","explanation":"Spread describes how much values differ. A typical value alone cannot show whether observations are tightly clustered or highly varied.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Understand samples and selection bias","explanation":"A sample may not represent the population you care about. Inspect how data was selected before extending a conclusion to everyone.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Distinguish association from causation","explanation":"An observed association does not establish that one variable causes another. Confounding and chance are alternative explanations.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Represent uncertainty honestly","explanation":"Uncertainty belongs in the explanation of a result. Avoid presenting an estimate as an exact fact about every future observation.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Match conclusions to the available evidence","explanation":"Match the conclusion to the analysis and available evidence. Explain what remains unknown and what additional data would help.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"data-visualisation","name":"Data Visualisation","category":"Data","description":"Communicate data with clear and honest charts.","project":"Create a small dashboard from a fictional business dataset.","question":"When is a line chart appropriate?","answer":"When showing an ordered sequence, often change over time.","lessons":[{"title":"Start with the question the chart answers","explanation":"A chart should answer a specific question. Decide whether the reader needs a comparison, distribution, relationship or trend.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Choose a suitable visual representation","explanation":"Different representations emphasise different patterns. Choose the one that makes the intended comparison straightforward.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Label units, axes and categories","explanation":"Labels should state what values and units mean. A chart that requires the reader to guess is difficult to interpret correctly.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Use colour to clarify meaning","explanation":"Colour can identify categories or draw attention, but should not be the only way to distinguish important information.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Avoid misleading scales or comparisons","explanation":"Scales and axes affect perceived differences. Avoid hiding context or using a scale that exaggerates the intended conclusion.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Review the chart alongside the underlying values","explanation":"Compare the final visual with the underlying data. Check whether labels, totals and annotations support the same honest interpretation.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"java","name":"Java","category":"Backend","description":"Build a structured command-line application.","project":"Build a fictional inventory command-line tool.","question":"Why handle exceptions deliberately?","answer":"To give meaningful recovery or failure behaviour instead of hiding errors.","lessons":[{"title":"Understand values, control flow and types","explanation":"Types and control flow establish what a Java program can represent and how it decides what to do. Use clear names and small examples while learning.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Organise logic into classes and methods","explanation":"Classes and methods organise responsibilities. Keep a method’s purpose clear and avoid storing unrelated behaviour in one class.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Use collections for practical tasks","explanation":"Collections support common operations on groups of values. Choose a suitable collection based on ordering, uniqueness and lookup needs.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Read data and handle exceptions","explanation":"File or network work can fail for reasons outside the program’s logic. Handle the error with a useful message or deliberate recovery.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Write checks for application behaviour","explanation":"Tests should verify meaningful outcomes and boundary cases. A passing demonstration alone does not cover every supported input.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document building and running the project","explanation":"Document the build and run process with a sample command. A reviewer should be able to reproduce the tool without guessing its environment.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"cplusplus","name":"C++","category":"Backend","description":"Write predictable programs with careful resource handling.","project":"Build a small command-line data-processing project.","question":"What does RAII help manage?","answer":"Resources whose lifetime should follow the lifetime of an owning object.","lessons":[{"title":"Understand types, control flow and functions","explanation":"Explicit types and functions help express what a program expects and returns. Read compiler diagnostics as information about the contract you are violating.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Use standard containers and algorithms","explanation":"Standard containers and algorithms cover many routine tasks. Prefer a suitable established abstraction to a custom data structure without a clear need.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Organise behaviour through classes","explanation":"Classes should own a coherent responsibility and protect their invariants. Avoid exposing state that can be changed into an invalid configuration.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Manage resource lifetime deliberately","explanation":"Resource lifetime needs careful ownership. RAII ties cleanup to an owning object’s lifetime so ordinary control flow releases the resource.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Inspect compiler warnings and errors","explanation":"Warnings can reveal conversions, unused values or other suspicious behaviour. Investigate them rather than suppressing them without understanding.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test boundary cases and document build steps","explanation":"Boundary tests help reveal invalid memory assumptions and unexpected inputs. Record compiler settings and run steps so the result is reproducible.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"go","name":"Go","category":"Backend","description":"Build a small service with clear interfaces and error handling.","project":"Create a small HTTP service with validation and tests.","question":"Why handle errors explicitly?","answer":"It makes failure paths visible and supports deliberate recovery.","lessons":[{"title":"Use types and functions to express behaviour","explanation":"Types and functions give a Go program a clear structure. Keep each operation understandable through its inputs, result and errors.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Organise a project into packages","explanation":"Packages group related behaviour behind explicit names. Organise dependencies so it is clear which layer is responsible for an operation.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Handle errors explicitly","explanation":"Go’s explicit error values make failure paths visible. Check them and choose whether to recover, add context or return the failure.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Build HTTP handlers and validate requests","explanation":"HTTP handlers must validate the request and return predictable responses. Keep status codes and error messages consistent.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Use concurrency only where needed","explanation":"Concurrency introduces coordination as well as parallel work. Use it for a defined benefit and plan cancellation or completion.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Test and document the running service","explanation":"Tests and documentation should show how the service behaves and how to run it. Include rejected requests, not just the successful example.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"rust","name":"Rust","category":"Backend","description":"Use ownership and types to write dependable application logic.","project":"Build a command-line tool that validates a sample file.","question":"Why use Result for recoverable errors?","answer":"It makes success and failure explicit in the function’s contract.","lessons":[{"title":"Understand ownership and borrowing","explanation":"Ownership describes which value is responsible for data, while borrowing permits access under rules checked by the compiler. Use simple examples to understand those relationships.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Model values using structs and enums","explanation":"Structs and enums model data and alternatives explicitly. Prefer a representation that prevents unsupported states where practical.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Handle recoverable errors with Result","explanation":"Result expresses either a successful value or a recoverable error. Callers must deliberately handle or propagate the failure.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Work with collections and iterators","explanation":"Iterators organise operations over collections. Choose transformations that make the data flow easier to understand.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Use modules and tests to organise the project","explanation":"Modules separate responsibilities; tests verify the behaviour those modules expose. Keep important boundaries clear.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Document how to build and run a small tool","explanation":"A command-line tool needs clear inputs, output and error messages. Document building and running it with a sample file that contains no private data.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]},{"slug":"html-css","name":"HTML & CSS","category":"Frontend","description":"Build accessible page structure and responsive layouts.","project":"Build a responsive landing page with a working navigation structure.","question":"Why use semantic HTML?","answer":"It gives content meaningful structure for browsers and assistive technology.","lessons":[{"title":"Use semantic landmarks and headings","explanation":"Semantic elements communicate the role of page content. Landmarks and a sensible heading structure help people navigate and understand the page.","exercise":"Write a short note identifying where this concept appears in your practice project."},{"title":"Create labelled, keyboard-friendly forms","explanation":"Labels connect controls with their meaning. Use real buttons and inputs so keyboard and assistive-technology behaviour is available.","exercise":"Apply this idea to one small part of your project and record the change."},{"title":"Understand the box model and sizing","explanation":"The box model describes content, padding, border and margin. Understanding sizing avoids unexpected overflow when a layout becomes narrower.","exercise":"Create a normal example and a failure or boundary example for this concept."},{"title":"Arrange content with Flexbox and Grid","explanation":"Flexbox and Grid solve different layout arrangements. Choose an approach that matches the relationship between elements rather than forcing fixed positions.","exercise":"Explain your implementation to someone who has not seen the project."},{"title":"Adapt layouts to smaller screens","explanation":"Responsive design rearranges content to fit available space. Test long text and forms as well as the ideal screenshot.","exercise":"Check the result against the expected behaviour and record any limitation."},{"title":"Check readability, contrast and focus states","explanation":"Check contrast, readable text and visible focus states on the actual page. Accessibility depends on usable behaviour as well as the presence of attributes.","exercise":"Document what you learned and what you would improve next."}]}];
